  "domain": "blockchain",
  "definition": "A preimage is an input value that, when passed through a cryptographic hash function, produces a specified output digest. Preimage resistance, the computational infeasibility of finding such an input given only the digest, is a core security property required of cryptographic hash functions. In protocols such as hash time-locked contracts, revealing the preimage of a previously published hash is used as cryptographic proof that a condition has been satisfied, enabling trustless conditional payments.",
